\pard\li852 I.e., 0 precedes 10 which precedes 11x, and 110 and 111 are lexicographically consecutive.\par
Given this rule, we can define the Huffman code for an alphabet just by giving the bit lengths of the codes for each symbol of the alphabet in order; this is sufficient to determite the actual codes. In our example, the code is completely defined by the sequence of bit lengths (2, 1, 3, 3). The following algorithm generates the codes as integers, inteded to be read from most- to least-significant bit. The code lengths are initially in tree[I].Len; the codes are produces in tree[I].Code.\par
1) Count the number of codes for each code length. Let bl_count[N] be the number of codes of lengths N, N >= 1.\par
2) Fing the numerical value of the smallest code for each code length :\par
\trowd\trgaph72\trleft1136\trpaddl72\trpaddr72\trpaddfl3\trpaddfr3
\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx5289 
\pard\intbl\f3 code = 0;\par
bl_count[0] = 0;\par
for (bits = 1; bits <= MAX_BITS; bits++) \{\par
    code = (code + bl_count[bits-1]) << 1;\par
    next_code[bits] = code;\par
\}\f0\cell\row 
\pard\li852 3) Assign numerical values to all codes, using consecutive values for all codes of the same length with the base values determined at step 2. Codes that are never used (which have a bit length of zero) must be assigned a value.\par
\trowd\trgaph72\trleft1136\trpaddl72\trpaddr72\trpaddfl3\trpaddfr3
\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx6159 
\pard\intbl\f3 for (n = 0; n <= max_code; n++) \{\par
    len = tree[n].Len;\par
    if (len !- 0) \{\par
        tree[n].Code = next_code[len];\par
        next_code[len]++;\par
    \}\par
\}\f0\cell\row 
\pard\li852\sl240\slmult1 Example :\par
Consider the alphabet ABCDEFGH, with bit lengths (3, 3, 3, 3, 3, 2, 4, 4). After step 1, we have :\par
