\pard\li568\sl240\slmult1 3.2.3. Details of block format\par

\pard\li852\sl240\slmult1 Each block of compressed data begins with 3 header bits containing the following data :\par
\trowd\trgaph72\trleft1136\trpaddl72\trpaddr72\trpaddfl3\trpaddfr3
\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx2128\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx2913 
\pard\intbl first bit\cell BFINAL\cell\row\trowd\trgaph72\trleft1136\trpaddl72\trpaddr72\trpaddfl3\trpaddfr3
\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx2128\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx2913 
\pard\intbl next 2 bits\cell BTYPE\cell\row 
\pard\li852\sl240\slmult1 Note that the header bits do not necessarily begin on a byte boundaty, since a block does not necessarily occupy an integral number of bytes.\par
BFINAL id set if and ony if this is the last block of the data set.\par
BTYPE specifies how the data are compressed, as follows :\par

\pard\li1136\sl240\slmult1 00 - no compression\par
01 - compressed with fixed Huffman codes\par
10 - compressed with dynamic Huffman codes\par
11 - reserved (error)\par

\pard\li852\sl240\slmult1 The only difference between the two compressed cases is how the Huffman codes for the literal/length and distance alphabets are defines.\par
In all cases, the decoding algorithm for the actual data is as follows :\par
\trowd\trgaph72\trleft1136\trpaddl72\trpaddr72\trpaddfl3\trpaddfr3
\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx7884 
\pard\intbl\f3 do\par
  read block header from input stream.\par
  if stored with no compression\par
    skip any remaining bits in currently partially processed byte\par
    read LEN and NLEN (see next section)\par
    copy LEN bytes of data to output\par
  otherwise\par
    if compressed with dynamic Huffman codes\par
      read representation of code trees (see subsection below)\par
    loop (until end of block code recognized)\par
      decode literal/length value from input stream\par
      if value < 256\par
        copy value (literal byte) to output stream\par
      otherwise\par
        if value = end of block (256)\par
          break from loop\par
        otherwise\par
          decode distance from input stream\par
          move backwards distance bytes in the output stream and copy length bytes from this position to the output stream.\par
      end loop\par
while not last block\f0\cell\row 
\pard\li852\sl240\slmult1 Note that a duplicated string reference may refer to a string in a previous block; i.e., the backward distance may cross one or more block boundaries. However a distance cannot refer past the beginning of the output stream. (An application using a preset dictionary might discard part of the output stream; a distance can refer to that part of the output stream anuway) Note also that the referenced string may overlap the current position; for example, if the last 2 bytes decoded have values X and Y, a string reference with <length = 5, distance = 2> adds X,Y,X,Y,X to the output stream.\par
We now specify each conmpression method in turn.\par

\pard\li568\sl240\slmult1 3.2.4. Non-compressed block\par

\pard\li852\sl240\slmult1 Any bits of input up to the next byte boundary are ignored. The rest of the block consists of the following information :\par
\trowd\trgaph72\trleft1136\trpaddl72\trpaddr72\trpaddfl3\trpaddfr3
\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx1666\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx2361\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx4766 
\pard\intbl 0 1\par
LEN\cell 2 3\par
NLEN\cell 4...\par
... LEN bytes of literal data...\cell\row 
\pard\li852\sl240\slmult1 LEN is the number of data bytes in the block. NLEN is the one's compelemnt of LEN.\par

\pard\li568\sl240\slmult1 3.2.5. Compressed blocks (length and distance codes)\par

\pard\li852\sl240\slmult1 As noted above, encoded data blocks in the "deflate" format consist of sequences of symbols drawn from three condeptually distinct alphabers : either literal butes, from the alphabet of byte values (0..255), or <length, backward distance> pairs, where the length is drawn from (3..258) and the distance is drawn from (1..32,768). In fact, the literal and length alphabets are merged into a single alphabet (0..285), where values 0..255 represent literal bytes, the value 256 indicates end-of-block, and values 257..285 represent length codes (possibly in conjuction with extra bits following the symbol code) as follows :\par
\trowd\trgaph72\trleft1136\trpaddl72\trpaddr72\trpaddfl3\trpaddfr3
